<p>Israel said on April 19 that it had instructed its military to use &quot;full force&quot; against what it described as imminent threats in Lebanon, despite a truce that took effect on April 17. Israeli officials also said homes along the border that they allege Hezbollah used would be demolished. The move highlights how fragile the ceasefire remains, even as it has paused weeks of fighting between Israel and the Iran-backed Hezbollah movement. Some displaced residents in south Lebanon have started returning home, while many others are still holding back because they are unsure the truce will last. The latest statements suggest that, while the ceasefire has opened a narrow path to calm, military operations and planned demolitions near the border are still shaping life on the ground.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/RGUr9QY3WJLEJh9iQABu82XBAEu-VKJqjYkJ_YKqA2t3gePNc9YHGiepO60CykAYJvXpC4Nz1QUFbQpE-ZSXwW2PX7T83zbgDjBtpDmoV_Iq8lQWpcW4CK2DLfGZghhyuPTcD5Vx4lHRVB7R-znyjl9ur1dKzm_avqF4aLacnuWcmRDTtUykZpovfmUGtw8O0D0HtX59ORMq-qyRvD2Hc51-hFd_ikqGpdU4x44iemQdUMtntN2zr2ezz-I8ZjzKxP8QqA' alt='Israeli military vehicles on the Israeli side of the Israel-Lebanon border, after a 10-day ceasefire between Lebanon and Israel went into effect, April 17, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Demolitions continue: Israeli state media said on April 19 that demolitions were under way in south Lebanon, showing that activity continued after the truce began.</li><li>Security zone logic: Katz has repeatedly cast house demolitions along the border as part of a broader effort to create a security zone in southern Lebanon.</li><li>Public map signal: On Sunday, the Israeli military published a map showing a red-marked belt along the south Lebanon border, publicly identifying the area where it said troops were operating.</li></ul><blockquote>Full force, both on the ground and from the air - Israel Katz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Israeli government: Defense Minister Israel 